  "source": {
    "title": "Armagh Guardian, 11 June 1920, p. 3 (county council count, area by area); first preferences also in the Newry Telegraph, 8 June 1920, p. 5, and the Frontier Sentinel, 12 June 1920, p. 5",
    "publisher": "Armagh Guardian (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "first preferences add to the printed valid poll; quota = valid / (seats + 1) + 1 as printed; each surplus equals the elected candidate's total less the quota and equals the printed transfers (plus papers with no further preference); each count is the previous count plus the transfers; every figure read from the page images"
  },
  "note": "Proportional representation (single transferable vote) under the Local Government (Ireland) Act 1919; polled Saturday 5 June 1920 and counted at Armagh by T. E. Reid, secretary of the county council and returning officer. Forkhill electoral area (five seats): the electoral divisions of Armaghbrague, Ballybot, Ballymyre, Belleek, Camlough, Camly, Cloghoge, Creggan Lower, Crossmaglen, Cullyhanna, Dorsy, Forkhill, Jonesborough, Killevy, Latbirget, Lisleitrim, Moybane and Newtownhamilton. 5,953 valid votes; quota 993. Elected in the order Aiken, Calvert, M'Ardle, M'Guill, Murphy; Murphy, the higher of the two remaining candidates, was declared elected without a quota. Papers with no further preference are those the paper calls plumped votes: Calvert 228, M'Parland 41, O'Neill 53. The Armagh Guardian prints O'Neill's share of M'Parland's votes as 1 but his new total as 441; 11 is used, which fits that total and the 41 plumped votes. Peter Mackin withdrew his nomination."
}
